NAME
    bt - display or reset the live call-stack backtrace

SYNOPSIS
    bt [<count>]
    bt reset
    bt <cpu> [<count>|reset]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the inferred call chain currently on the stack, innermost frame
    first, showing the called target (resolved via label(1)), call site,
    and frame number. Frames are tracked from call/return opcodes and
    interrupt entries (NMI and IRQ frames are labelled as interrupts).

    Because the call stack is inferred from control-flow seams rather than
    read from a frame pointer, routines that manually push/pop return
    addresses or rewrite the stack pointer can cause drift. Rising
    'unmatched returns' counts or an ever-growing depth indicate stack
    drift; run 'bt reset' to resynchronize before relying on 'step out' or
    profile(1). Stack depth is capped to prevent unbounded growth. Frames
    entered before the debug session attached are not tracked.

    Prefixing a processor name from cpus(1) inspects that processor's call
    stack. Only processors advertising 'bt' in cpus(1) maintain a call
    stack (for example, main CPU and SA-1 on SNES, not GSU or SPC700).

OPTIONS
    <count>
        Maximum number of stack frames to print.

    reset
        Discard the tracked call chain and start fresh from the current
        instruction.

EXAMPLES
    bt
    bt 8
    bt reset
    bt sa1

EXIT STATUS
    0   Backtrace printed or reset.
    1   No target attached, processor has no call stack, or session fault.

SEE ALSO
    cpus(1), callers(1), step(1), profile(1), bp(1), label(1)
